@grammar "dangling_else"
@root stmt
@engine glr
@skip WS

; The textbook "dangling else" ambiguity every parser-generator course
; uses -- real, present in C/Java/JavaScript/PHP's own grammars, unlike
; `test/ambig_tiebreak/ambig_tiebreak.aether`'s deliberately abstract
; "x matches rule a or rule b": `if c1 then if c2 then s1 else s2` --
; does `else s2` belong to the inner `if c2` or the outer `if c1`?
;
; This grammar is genuinely ambiguous at the CFG level: a real
; shift/reduce conflict. Having just reduced the inner `IF COND THEN
; stmt`, and seeing `ELSE` next, the table can either shift it (attach
; to the inner if) or reduce first (closing the inner if without an
; else, leaving `ELSE` free for the *outer* if instead). Resolved
; exactly the way real yacc/bison-generated parsers resolve it, with no
; special-casing needed for this grammar at all:
; `Grammar.LRTable.Automaton`'s canonical "shift before reduce"
; conflict-cell ordering means `Grammar.GLR`'s own declared-order
; tie-break always prefers the shift, attaching `else` to the nearest
; still-open `if`.

WS    := [ \t\n]+
IF    := "if"
THEN  := "then"
ELSE  := "else"
COND  := [a-z]
OTHER := [0-9]

stmt    := if_stmt | OTHER
if_stmt := IF cond:COND THEN then_branch:stmt (ELSE else_branch:stmt)?
